00:02:43,502 --> 00:02:49,000
as a motivation to get into those apps and developing those apps

52
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
So, yeah

53
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Now, just briefly about what is the Swift Student Challenge

54
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
This is the main topic for today

55
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:05,000
So, the Swift Student Challenge is an annual coding competition hosted by Apple for students

56
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:14,000
The challenge encourages students to showcase their coding skills by creating an interactive and creative Swift playground project

57
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,734
So, if you never heard about the Swift Student Challenge, you might be thinking

58
00:03:17,734 --> 00:03:25,384
oh, maybe I can build a social media app or like a live streaming app or even like a music player app

59
00:03:25,384 --> 00:03:31,000
But there are some important constraints we have to mind

60
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:40,000
First of all, the app has to be submitted in a zip file, and the file can't be over 25 MB

61
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Second, the whole core experience of the app has to be done in under three minutes

62
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:55,000
And third, the whole app experience, the whole playground has to be completely offline

63
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:01,565
So, with those constraints in mind, I submitted my first app in 2025

64
00:04:01,565 --> 00:04:05,348
Last year, it was called NeoMnemo

65
00:04:05,348 --> 00:04:09,000
This app, the idea behind this app was really simple

66
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,389
So, you would just type a keyword you wanted to memorize

67
00:04:13,389 --> 00:04:19,532
and then you would, like, imagine a story in your mind to help you memorize that keyword

68
00:04:19,532 --> 00:04:27,451
And I used the image playground API to generate an image based on the concept you wanted to memorize

69
00:04:28,014 --> 00:04:35,907
So, this was basically a flashcards app that would help you memorize some abstract or hard-to-memorize concepts

70
00:04:35,907 --> 00:04:40,939
And, yeah, that idea was based on my personal story

71
00:04:40,939 --> 00:04:47,463
When I was going to enter university in Japan, I had to study Japanese history

72
00:04:47,463 --> 00:04:50,420
And that was really hard for me because I grew up in Brazil

73
00:04:50,420 --> 00:04:52,623
My whole life I spent in Brazil

74
00:04:52,623 --> 00:04:59,619
So, studying Japanese history was really hard, especially to memorize like people's name, event's name and dates

75
00:04:59,619 --> 00:05:07,000
So, I decided to create short stories to kind of like help me memorize those concepts in Japanese history

76
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:12,516
And this method was really effective because in the entrance exam for my university in Japan

77
00:05:12,516 --> 00:05:14,965
I scored like 98 out of 100

78
00:05:14,965 --> 00:05:21,809
So, this was a pretty good methodology that I used to study Japanese history

79
00:05:21,809 --> 00:05:27,230
But unfortunately, my app NeoMnemo ended up not winning last year

80
00:05:27,230 --> 00:05:31,880
And those are some of the reasons why I think my app didn't win

81
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:33,000
The first one

82
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:39,259
So, as you guys just saw the app was kind of like a good tool to use

83
00:05:39,259 --> 00:05:41,535
But yeah, it was just a tool

84
00:05:41,535 --> 00:05:50,734
It wasn't like some kind of experience that people could like get to know more about myself, my personal story

85
00:05:50,734 --> 00:05:52,774
or like who I am

86
00:05:52,774 --> 00:05:57,862
So, this was the first reason why I think I didn't get to win last year

87
00:05:57,862 --> 00:06:06,000
And the second one was that even though the concept that inspired me to make this app was based on a personal story

88
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:12,687
that didn't reflect in the app's design and implementation and interactions

89
00:06:12,687 --> 00:06:18,316
So, those are the reasons why I think I didn't win last year

90
00:06:18,316 --> 00:06:27,642
This year, I decided to try once again to participate into the challenge for my second time with my app called Diary

91
00:06:28,072 --> 00:06:36,740
And, basically, the inspiration behind this app was that last year I went back to my hometown, São Paulo in Brazil

92
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:45,636
And when I was in my home. My dad, he came to me and he was holding, like three diaries from my grandfather

93
00:06:45,636 --> 00:06:50,142
And he asked me, Can you read those diaries? They're all in Japanese

94
00:06:50,142 --> 00:06:52,000
Maybe you can read them

95
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:58,229
And then I looked at the diaries. I can speak Japanese, modern Japanese

96
00:06:58,229 --> 00:07:03,438
but those diaries, they were written in old Japanese

97
00:07:03,438 --> 00:07:10,132
So, the Japanese language passed through a language reform in the 50s

98
00:07:10,132 --> 00:07:13,000
and all the characters in Japanese changed

99
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
So, they became kind of like simplified

100
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:21,265
And that gave me an idea to make an app

101
00:07:21,265 --> 00:07:26,996
So, I wanted to combine all those elements

102
00:07:26,996 --> 00:07:34,688
my personal story, language learning, and history of Japanese immigration to Brazil

103
00:07:34,688 --> 00:07:38,437
So, with all those concepts in mind

104
00:07:38,437 --> 00:07:45,443
I decided to create an experience to help Japanese Brazilians rediscover their roots

105
00:07:45,443 --> 00:07:56,758
And while doing that, they would also learn the history of how the Japanese language changed in those 100 years

106
00:07:56,758 --> 00:08:03,265
So, I decided to make an initial prototype for this app, and it looked like this

107
00:08:03,265 --> 00:08:07,000
First, the data I used for the initial prototype

108
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:12,662
I decided to look for a conversion table between kyūjitai and shinjitai

109
00:08:12,662 --> 00:08:17,915
In Chinese, it would be something like the traditional Chinese and simplified Chinese

110
00:08:17,915 --> 00:08:24,883
And I just looked it up on GitHub and found this JSON table in a repository called Kyujipy

111
00:08:24,883 --> 00:08:31,669
And yeah, I decided to build a really simple prototype for my first initial version of my app

112
00:08:31,669 --> 00:08:38,000
It was basically a phrase in Japanese would appear containing the old character over there

113
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:48,773
And users would read the phrase, look for the corresponding character in modern Japanese, and select the answer

114
00:08:48,773 --> 00:08:52,715
And I even tried to add difficulty to my app

115
00:08:52,715 --> 00:09:00,499
so characters with similar radicals or similar components would appear in the hard version of the app

116
00:09:01,657 --> 00:09:03,319
But there was a problem

117
00:09:03,319 --> 00:09:06,370
This app was still not a whole experience

118
00:09:06,370 --> 00:09:11,000
It felt more like a test or an exam or a learning tool

119
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:15,000
And it wasn't like an immersive experience yet

120
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:20,938
So yeah, from that point on, I decided to convert this game,

121
00:09:20,938 --> 00:09:28,755
This kind of like test like experience into an immersive experience, an immersive game

122
00:09:28,755 --> 00:09:34,289
So, I started by building my onboarding screen, and it looked like this

123
00:09:34,289 --> 00:09:36,718
I first introduced my background, who I am

124
00:09:36,718 --> 00:09:38,000
I'm Japanese-Brazilian

125
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:43,000
And then one day, I decided to go into my family's basement

126
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,300
And in the basement, when you enter, there are some objects up here

127
00:09:47,300 --> 00:09:54,000
And users have to use their fingers to move the objects around and review the diary behind it

128
00:09:54,000 --> 00:10:02,000
And for giving kind of like a mysterious look to the diary, I decided to use a shader, a metal shader

129
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:07,000
I used Paul Hudson's Inferno to add this effect

130
00:10:07,350 --> 00:10:10,350
And also, I don't know if you guys know this game

131
00:10:10,350 --> 00:10:12,557
I really like it. It's one of my favorite games

132
00:10:12,557 --> 00:10:14,744
It's called Chrono Trigger

133
00:10:14,744 --> 00:10:25,000
And I decided to add kind of like an effect for the time-traveling experience when you enter the diary and go to the past

134
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:30,000
So, this is just one of my inspirations for creating this theme

135
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,090
And once you get into the diary

136
00:10:32,090 --> 00:10:37,003
you can start reading the entries that my grandfather wrote

137
00:10:37,003 --> 00:10:45,361
and like review the modern versions of all the old Japanese characters that appears in the sentence

138
00:10:45,361 --> 00:10:48,000
So, the app now felt more like this

139
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:52,000
You would see the old form and the modern form of the characters

140
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:58,280
And also, at the same time, you would be learning the history of Japanese immigration to Brazil

141
00:10:58,280 --> 00:11:02,000
because all the entries are based on real entries

142
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:09,000
And how I did that was by actually going outside visiting museums

143
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:15,000
and also reading a lot of documents based on Japanese immigration

144
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:23,000
So, one of the places I visited was the Yokohama Japanese Overseas Migration Museum

145
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:24,000
It's in Yokohama

146
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
And I read lots of documents

147
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:32,000
So, you guys can see some of the images of the museum right here

148
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:37,000
I read some of those books to get to know more about Japanese immigration

149
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:41,000
And the final experience looked like this

150
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:46,000
So, a phrase in old Japanese based on those documents would appear

151
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:47,000
Users would tap the phrase

152
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:53,000
And in the top, I inserted a panel that would automatically scroll

153
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:58,000
and review the modern versions of the characters in Japanese

154
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,000
So, that's how the whole experience looks like

155
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,000
When you finish, there is an English translation that appears in the panel

156
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:14,000
And I also added a glowing effect to the panel when you finish all the characters in a certain page

157
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:20,000
So, that's the comparison between the initial prototype that I made in the beginning

158
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,000
and the final experience

159
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:26,500
So yeah, by creating this app

160
00:12:26,500 --> 00:12:30,000
I could combine all of the elements I wanted to combine

161
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:35,289
my personal roots, my story, immigration, Japanese immigration history

162
00:12:35,289 --> 00:12:37,535
and also language learning

163
00:12:37,535 --> 00:12:42,000
So, the final experience looked like this

164
00:12:42,765 --> 00:12:45,461
And with that app

165
00:12:45,461 --> 00:12:51,237
I won the Swift Student Challenge this year and received the certificate

166
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:55,000
Thank you so much

167
00:12:55,000 --> 00:13:02,000
And finally, I'd like to close this talk by telling you guys some of the things that I learned

168
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:05,000
by trying the challenge this year

169
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:10,000
And if some of you are thinking about joining the challenge, just do it

170
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:16,360
Because this year I was going to do an internship at a company

171
00:13:16,360 --> 00:13:20,500
so I wasn't thinking about joining the challenge, even though I'm a student

172
00:13:20,500 --> 00:13:24,949
Because last year I didn't win, and I was kind of not motivated to join

173
00:13:24,949 --> 00:13:30,000
But then I decided to visit some community events in Japan

174
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:33,472
and this was one of the best experiences in my life

175
00:13:33,472 --> 00:13:37,000
because it was a great experience to learn more about myself

176
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:43,924
to build an actual meaningful application

177
00:13:43,924 --> 00:13:48,960
And also, if you guys are thinking about building something for your community

178
00:13:48,960 --> 00:13:51,696
for your family members or even for yourself

179
00:13:51,696 --> 00:13:55,445
just do it because you will learn a lot about yourself

180
00:13:55,445 --> 00:14:00,618
You might help a family member or even someone from your community

181
00:14:00,618 --> 00:14:03,401
So, I'd like to close with this phrase

182
00:14:03,401 --> 00:14:08,738
Sometimes the best idea is not always the biggest idea

183
00:14:08,738 --> 00:14:14,000
Sometimes the best idea is the one that only you could have made

184
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:16,000
So, thank you so much for listening

185
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
And those are my socials

186
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:23,000
If you guys want to scan and follow me on any of the social media platforms

187
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:24,000
feel free to do so

188
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:25,037
Thank you so much
